.\" UNIX V7 source code: see /COPYRIGHT or www.tuhs.org for details.
.TH FCLOSE 3S
.SH NAME
fclose, fflush \- close or flush a stream
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <stdio.h>
.PP
.B int fclose(FILE *stream);
.PP
.B int fflush(FILE *stream);
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Fclose
causes any buffers for the named
.I stream
to be emptied, and the file to be closed.
Buffers allocated by the standard input/output system
are freed.
.PP
.I Fclose
is performed automatically upon
calling
.IR  exit (3).
.B Note:
v7's page said
.IR exit (2).
It is
.IR exit (3)
that does it \-
.IR _exit (2)
flushes nothing, which is the whole difference between the two.
.PP
.I Fflush
causes any buffered data for the named output
.I stream
to be written to that file.
The stream remains open.
.PP
.B Note:
a null
.I stream
flushes every open output stream.  That is C11 sec 7.21.5.2; v7 had no such case.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
close(2),
fopen(3),
setbuf(3),
exit(3),
stdio(3)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
These routines return
.SM
.B EOF
if
.I stream
is not associated with an output file, or
if buffered data cannot be transferred to that file.
.SH BESM-6 NOTES
A line-buffered stream is held at
.B _cnt == 0
(see
.IR stdio (3)),
and
.I fflush
must
.B leave it there.
Restoring a real count would send the next
.I putc
down the fast path and lose the newline flush.
.PP
The cleanup that
.IR exit (3)
runs is reached through a
.I pointer
that this file's
.IR _flsbuf ()
arms on the first buffered write, never by name.  Naming it would put the whole
of stdio into every program ever linked, because
.I crt0
tail-jumps to
.IR exit :
the
.B hello
program measured 2255 words that way and 100 through the pointer.  See
.IR exit (3).
.SH FILES
share/besm6/lib/libc.a
.br
lib/libc/stdio/flsbuf.c \- fflush, fclose and the cleanup share one file, as v7
keeps them
